###########################
#  Main text

BEGIN_TEXT
Recall two key definitions. Remember the second "definition" is really just an intuitive statement. $BR
\( \textbf{Definition:}\) A set \( S \subseteq \mathbb{R}^3 \) is a \( \textbf{surface} \) if there exists a simple regular 2-variable parametrization of \( S \).
	A set \( S \subseteq \mathbb{R}^3 \) is a \( \textbf{piecewise surface} \) if \( S \) is a finite union of surfaces glued together along their boundaries. $BR $BR


For each set \( S\subseteq \mathbb{R}^3 \), identify whether \(S \) is a surface, a piecewise surface, both, or neither. 
\( \textit{Hint:} \) Can you intuitively describe the entire surface "smoothly" with only two parameters? Think of folding and gluing a piece of paper to form the shape.

$BR 
1. A sphere
$BR
\{ image( "l1-sphere.png", width=>200, height=>200 ) \}

$BR

END_TEXT

